summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow-up of chronic problems.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Carolyn Smith is a 49-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for follow-up of her chronic problems. She is primarily concerned about her osteoarthritis, which has been flaring up in her elbows.

The patient reports that her osteoarthritis has been flaring up in her elbows, particularly her right elbow, and she is concerned about this. She notes that she is sitting at a desk all day at work and is wondering if this is contributing to the flare-ups. She denies any other joint pain. She occasionally experiences numbness in her hands when typing for long periods of time, which she tries to relieve by shaking out her arms. She has had a history of gout, but her last episode was 3 months ago and she has not had any flare-ups since. She has had psoriasis, which has been well controlled on current medications. She has been using clobetasol for her scalp and notes that this has been doing well.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports pain and swelling in her elbows.
Skin: Denies any other skin lesions.

PHYSICAL EXAMINATION

Neurological: Alert and oriented. No focal deficits.

Musculoskeletal: Examination of the right elbow reveals edema and inflammation of the olecranon, tenderness to palpation, and pain with pronation and supination. Pain with flexion and extension of the right arm.

RESULTS

Autoimmune panel: Negative.

ESR: 2.0 mg/L.

Uric acid: 4.2 mg/dL.

VITALS

Blood pressure: 128/72 mmHg.
Heart rate: 72 bpm.
Respiratory rate: 18 breaths per minute.
Temperature: 98.6°F.
Oxygen saturation: 98% on room air.

ASSESSMENT

Osteoarthritis of the right elbow.

PLAN

I have discussed the patient's symptoms and current medications with her today. She is currently on colchicine 0.6 mg twice daily as needed for flare-ups of her gout. We will continue her on this medication. For her osteoarthritis, we will order an x-ray of her right elbow. We will also prescribe meloxicam 15 mg once daily for pain and swelling. We will wait to see the x-ray results before referring her for physical therapy. From a psoriasis standpoint, everything is stable. We will continue her on the steroid cream for her scalp.

INSTRUCTIONS

We will order an x-ray of her right elbow. She will continue to take colchicine 0.6 mg twice daily as needed for flare-ups of her gout. We will also prescribe meloxicam 15 mg once daily for pain and swelling. We will wait to see the x-ray results before referring her for physical therapy. She will continue to use the steroid cream for her scalp.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>
