summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Left elbow pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Anna M. Rodriguez is a 26-year-old female with a past medical history significant for epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease who presents to the clinic with left elbow pain. The patient reports that her elbow has been stiff and painful, particularly after work, and she has difficulty opening and closing her left elbow. The pain is worse with use. She denies any numbness, tingling, or weakness in her hands. She reports a little bit of nasal congestion from allergies. She denies fever or chills. The patient reports that she has been doing well with her epilepsy, with her last seizure occurring one year ago. She is currently taking Keppra 500 mg twice a day. She also reports that she has been doing well with her depression, and she has been in therapy. She has been able to avoid medication since she is already on Keppra. She has been doing better with her depression, but she still feels stressed and down. She reports that she has not felt like hurting herself or anyone else. The patient denies any chest pain, shortness of breath, abdominal pain, nausea or vomiting.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Denies fever or chills.
• Musculoskeletal: Reports left elbow pain. Denies numbness, tingling, or weakness in the hands.
• Neurological: Denies seizures. Denies depression.
• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.
• Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain, nausea or vomiting. Endoscopy showed gastritis.
• ENT: Endorses nasal congestion.

PHYSICAL EXAMINATION

• General: Well-appearing female in no acute distress.
• Musculoskeletal: Examination of the left elbow: Erythema and edema of the left elbow. Pain to palpation of the olecranon. Warm to palpation. Pain with flexion and extension of the left elbow. Palpable left radial pulse.

RESULTS

• X-ray of the left elbow: No evidence of fracture.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

• Left elbow pain.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with left elbow pain, which is consistent with olecranon bursitis. She has a history of repetitive trauma to the area. I recommend a little pillow for her elbow to provide support. She will take meloxicam 15 mg once daily. She can ice the area. She will follow up in 2 weeks.
• Epilepsy.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient has been doing well with her epilepsy, with her last seizure occurring one year ago. She is currently taking Keppra 500 mg twice a day. She will continue this medication.
• Depression.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient has been doing well with her depression, but she still feels stressed and down. She has been in therapy. She has been able to avoid medication since she is already on Keppra. I would like to start her on Lexapro 10 mg once a day. We will see how she does with this medication.
• Peptic Ulcer Disease.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of peptic ulcer disease. She had an episode of gastritis on her endoscopy. She will continue on Protonix 40 mg once a day before meals.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>
