HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Christopher Watson is an 86-year-old male who returns for follow-up for adult hydrocephalus. He initially presented in May 2020 with 8-10 years of progressive gait impairment, cognitive impairment, and decreased bladder control. He underwent shunt surgery on August 1, 2020, and was placed on a Medtronic Strata programmable shunt in the ventricular peritoneal configuration, which was set at 2.0.

The patient reports that his walking has improved and he is able to walk to the dining room without assistance. However, he still has times where he needs help. His bladder control has improved but has leveled off, and he is now wearing diapers. He denies any issues with sores from wearing the diaper, or decreased or painful urination. He notes that his cognitive function has improved, stating he no longer feels lost when talking to someone. He denies any headaches or pain where the shunt is, and any abdominal pain.

PAST HISTORY

Adult hydrocephalus.

MEDICATIONS

Shunt.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Impaired ability to carry out daily functions. Denies fever or unintentional weight loss.
Genitourinary: Urinary incontinence and urgency. Denies genital sores, decreased or painful output.
Neurological: Continued walking impairment and cognitive impairment. Denies headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations.
Psychiatric: Denies depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.

VITALS

Blood pressure: 124/80.
Heart rate: 64.
Respirations: 18.
Weight: 174 pounds.
Pain: 0/10.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Constitutional: In no acute distress.
Neurological: Alert and oriented. Cranial nerves II-XII intact. No focal deficits.
Musculoskeletal: Ambulatory without assistive device. Gait score 6-8 out of 12, balance score 12 out of 16 for a total of 18 to 20 out of 28. Slightly improved from initial score of 15 to 17 out of 28.
Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.
Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm without murmurs, gallops, or rubs.

RESULTS

CT scan of the head from 10/15/2020 shows a frontal horn span at the level of the foramen of Monro of 4.6 cm with a third ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 mm. There is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdermal collection. This was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated any extra-axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.

ASSESSMENT

1. Adult hydrocephalus.
2. Gait impairment.
3. Urinary incontinence and urgency.
4. Cognitive impairment.

PLAN

The patient will follow up in 3 months. We will continue to monitor his improvement and will likely leave the shunt set at 2.0 for the next 2-3 months. The patient was advised that he may be able to drive again if his movement speed improves. He was also advised that he could enroll in a driver safety program without my recommendation.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>
