summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Tyler Nelson is a pleasant 74-year-old male who presents today for follow-up of his rheumatoid arthritis. He reports that his right knee pain has persisted since his last injection. He states that the injection provided relief for about 2 weeks, but his pain level has increased over the past 2 months. The pain is worse with prolonged sitting and getting up from a seated position. He reports pain in his right knee, which is also swollen. He describes his pain as 3/10 on a normal day, but notes that it can be as high as 6/10. He denies any fever or chills. He reports that he has been taking Xeljanz 11 mg daily and Celebrex 200 mg daily.

MEDICATIONS

Xeljanz 11 mg daily.
Celebrex 200 mg daily.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain, swelling, and stiffness.
Skin: No rashes or lesions noted.

PHYSICAL EXAMINATION

MSK: Examination of the right knee shows diminished range of motion. There is pitting edema around the knee and ankle. Normal capillary refill in the toes. The knee is warm. There is tenderness to palpation over the knee.

RESULTS

Right knee x-ray to evaluate for the cause of the swelling is pending.

ASSESSMENT

Rheumatoid arthritis.
Swollen right knee.

PLAN

We discussed the patient's symptoms and treatment options. I explained that I will give him another steroid injection and order an x-ray of his right knee. He will follow up with his primary care provider and we will set up physical therapy. If he does not respond to these interventions, he will need a surgery consult.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with his primary care provider. He will continue Xeljanz 11 mg daily and Celebrex 200 mg daily. He will try to reduce his salt intake and elevate his legs. He will also consider physical therapy.

PROCEDURE

Fluid aspiration and steroid injection procedure of the right knee. The risks, benefits, and alternatives were explained. The risks of steroid injection were explained, including but not limited to: hypopigmentation, soft tissue atrophy, tendon ruptures, and transient hypoglycemia, among others. Verbal consent was attained. The right knee was accessed through the lateral approach. 3 mL of initially clear fluid, then blood-tinged fluid. The right knee was prepped in a standard fashion. The right knee was injected with 80 mg of triamcinolone and 1 mL of lidocaine. The procedure was tolerated well with no complications. Post-procedure instructions were given. }}<|eot_id|>
