summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right upper quadrant pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Bobby Jackson is a pleasant 64-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right upper quadrant pain. The pain has been present for several weeks and was initially diagnosed as gallstones by Dr. Miller. He has been advised to avoid high-fat foods, but he has not had any improvement in his symptoms. He denies fevers, chills, or vomiting. He has nausea and right upper quadrant pain that is severe and occurs after meals. He rates his pain as an 8/10. He has a history of type 2 diabetes and has been diet controlled. He has been following a keto diet and has lost 25 pounds over the past 3 months. He enjoys photography and hiking. He has a family history of gallbladder disease.

MEDICAL HISTORY

Type 2 diabetes.

SURGICAL HISTORY

Gallbladder surgery.

SOCIAL HISTORY

He enjoys photography and hiking.

FAMILY HISTORY

Both parents had gallbladder surgery.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fevers, chills.
Gastrointestinal: Reports nausea, right upper quadrant pain.
Endocrine: Denies polyuria, polydipsia.
Musculoskeletal: Denies joint pain.

PHYSICAL EXAMINATION

Respiratory: Respiratory rate 16, no wheezing, rales, or rhonchi.
Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, rubs, or gallops.
Abdomen: Soft, no distension. Positive bowel sounds. Positive Murphy's sign. No rebound tenderness.

RESULTS

Ultrasound of the abdomen: Multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. Mild thickening of the gallbladder. Common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Bobby Jackson is a 64-year-old male who presents with a history of right upper quadrant pain. He was initially diagnosed with gallstones by Dr. Miller. He has been advised to avoid high-fat foods, but he has not had any improvement in his symptoms. He has a history of type 2 diabetes and has been diet controlled. He has been following a keto diet and has lost 25 pounds over the past 3 months. He enjoys photography and hiking. He has a family history of gallbladder disease.

Given the lack of improvement in his symptoms with dietary modifications, I believe the patient has mild cholecystitis with gallstones. I do not appreciate any frank obstruction, but his gallbladder is inflamed. Given this, I am recommending a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. This procedure will involve making a few small incisions and inserting a scope with instruments to remove the gallbladder. The procedure will take about an hour to an hour and a half. He will likely spend the night in the hospital for postoperative monitoring. He should avoid high-fat foods after surgery and eat foods that are high in fiber.

The patient has no questions about the assessment and plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

He will follow up in one week for postoperative care. He will see his primary care provider for diabetes follow-up. }}<|eot_id|>
