summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right shoulder pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Matthew Thompson is a 67-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of right shoulder pain. The patient reports that he injured his right shoulder playing football. He states that he was tackled and landed on his shoulder. The patient reports that he is right-handed. He denies numbness or tingling in his fingers. He states that he is able to grab a cup of coffee. However, he notes that he is having trouble lifting anything heavy. He denies any problems with the strength of his arm. He states that he has been taking Tylenol, but it has not provided significant relief. He reports that his symptoms are constant and have been present for the past 4 days.

The patient denies any neck pain. He denies any other joint pain in his right shoulder. He denies any other orthopedic injuries in the past. The patient reports that he had an appendectomy 3-4 years ago. He denies any other medical problems.

The patient denies fever or chills. He reports a slight headache. He denies loss of consciousness.

MEDICATIONS

Tylenol.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fever or chills.
Musculoskeletal: Reports right shoulder pain.
Neurological: Denies numbness or tingling in fingers.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Constitutional: Alert and in no acute distress.
Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.
Cardiovascular: Normal S1, S2. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs. No extra heart sounds.
Musculoskeletal: Examination of the right shoulder reveals pain to palpation of the posterior shoulder at the acromioclavicular joint. Decreased abduction and adduction to 90 degrees. Pain with supination and pronation of the right arm. Palpable radial pulse.

RESULTS

X-ray of the right shoulder reveals an acute acromioclavicular joint fracture.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Acute acromioclavicular joint fracture.

I have reviewed the patient's radiographs and discussed the findings with him. We have placed him in a sling. I have prescribed meloxicam 15 mg once a day. We will order a CT of the right shoulder. We will refer him to physical therapy to strengthen his shoulder in hopes of avoiding surgery in the future.

