Right lower back pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Jeremy Johnson is a pleasant 58-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of his right lower back pain. The pain began approximately 4 months ago while moving boxes at his son's college dorm. He reports pain in his lower back, particularly when he is sitting or moving around. He also notes numbness in his right foot. The pain has been present for the past 4 months, but has been worsening over the past 2 months. He has been unable to participate in his regular activities due to the pain. He reports he is able to walk, but it is very difficult. He has tried using ibuprofen, Tylenol, and ice packs for pain relief. He has not taken any muscle relaxants or opioids. He has also tried physical therapy at the gym, but it has not provided significant relief. He has not had any fevers or chills associated with the pain. He denies any weakness in his legs. He has some numbness in his right foot, but it resolves after 1 hour.

PAST HISTORY

The patient reports a past medical history significant for a total knee replacement of his right knee in the spring of 2020. He reports he has been doing well since the surgery and has been able to rehabilitate without any issues.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Neurological: Reports numbness in his right foot.
• Musculoskeletal: Reports pain in his lower back, particularly when he is sitting or moving around.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• Respiratory: Auscultation of the lungs is clear bilaterally.
• Cardiovascular: Auscultation of the heart is regular rate and rhythm without murmurs, gallops, or rubs.
• Neurological: No focal deficits noted. Positive straight leg raise and paraspinal muscle spasm.
• Musculoskeletal: Decreased flexion and extension of the lower back. No pain to palpation of the lumbar spine. Equal lower extremity strength bilaterally.

RESULTS

• Lumbar spine x-ray obtained on 10/14/2022, results were normal, no acute bony abnormality.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

• The patient presents with a history of right lower back pain. The patient reports pain in his lower back, particularly when he is sitting or moving around. He also reports numbness in his right foot. The pain has been present for the past 4 months, but has been worsening over the past 2 months. The patient has tried using ibuprofen, Tylenol, and ice packs for pain relief. He has not taken any muscle relaxants or opioids. He has also tried physical therapy at the gym, but it has not provided significant relief.
• The patient had a lumbar spine x-ray on 10/14/2022, which showed no acute bony abnormality.
• I believe the patient has an acute disk herniation.
• I have ordered a lumbar spine MRI to get a better look at what is going on.
• I am going to continue with physical therapy.
• I am also going to prescribe meloxicam 15 mg once a day.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 6 weeks to re-evaluate his symptoms and discuss the results of his lumbar spine MRI. If the patient continues to have symptoms, we can discuss the option of an epidural steroid injection to help take down some of the inflammation. }}<|eot_id|>
