summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right chest pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Julia Smith is a 55-year-old female who presents to the clinic for follow-up after an interior ST elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) where we placed another stent.

The patient reports that she was doing well after her last heart attack until she started having chest pain that would not go away. She went to the emergency room and was told she needed another stent.

She denies any chest pain or shortness of breath. She reports that she walks her dog every day, which is a mile and a half to two miles. She is excited for the upcoming fall season and does not have any plans for the holiday.

The patient has a history of coronary artery disease, hypertension, and diabetes. She has been experiencing some leg swelling since being discharged from the hospital. She is able to lie flat at night but uses a couple pillows. She has been able to afford her medications and is taking them as prescribed.

The patient reports that she has not been watching her salt intake as closely as she should, as she loves pizza and it is her favorite food. She is trying to watch her sugar intake because she does not want to go on insulin. She is taking metformin and trying to stay active by walking her dog.

MEDICATIONS

Aspirin 81 mg daily
Brilinta 90 mg twice daily
Lipitor 80 mg daily
Toprol 50 mg daily
Metformin
Lasix 40 mg daily
Aldactone 12.5 mg daily

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or shortness of breath.
Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.
Musculoskeletal: Denies leg swelling.
General: Denies fever, chills, or weight loss.
