summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Bilateral knee pain.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient reports that she has been working from home and does not have any other medical conditions. She is an active runner but has been unable to run for the past 6 months due to knee pain.

FAMILY HISTORY

The patient reports that she has never broken a bone, but she broke her arm in 6th grade.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fever, chills, weight loss.
Ears, Nose, Mouth, Throat: Denies hearing loss, dysphagia, dyspnea, dysphonia.
Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain, palpitations.
Respiratory: Denies cough, shortness of breath, wheezing.
Gastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, constipation.
Genitourinary: Denies dysuria, hematuria, changes in bowel habits.
Musculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain, knee creaking, limited mobility, decreased activity level.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Constitutional: In NCCU, alert and oriented.
Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs.
Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.
Musculoskeletal: Normal gait. Strength is 3/5 for abduction of legs bilaterally. Strength is 5/5 for the remainder of the lower extremities. Tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinacular retinaculum. Positive patellar grind test. No erythema, ecchymosis, or warmth of the skin appreciated. No effusion. Lachman, anterior and posterior drawer, and McMurray tests are negative bilaterally. Patella and achilles reflexes are symmetrical.

RESULTS

X-rays of the bilateral knees were obtained today and are normal, showing no fractures or osteoarthritis.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Bilateral knee pain.

Diagnosis: Patellofemoral pain syndrome.

Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with bilateral knee pain that is exacerbated by activity. She reports no specific injury, but denies any history of knee problems. The patient has normal gait, but decreased activity level. Her physical examination is positive for tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinacular retinaculum and positive patellar grind test. X-rays of the bilateral knees were normal. We have reviewed the results of her MRI and her symptoms are consistent with patellofemoral pain syndrome.

Treatment: The patient will continue taking ibuprofen or anti-inflammatory as needed. She will be referred to physical therapy for lower extremity exercises to increase strength and mobility, as well as to correct any incorrect running mechanics. She will refrain from running until further notice.

Patient Education and Counseling: I explained the diagnosis and treatment options to the patient. I advised her that she will need to take it easy from running for now and that we will continue to assess her progress with each visit. I also advised her that physical therapy will help with increasing her strength, mobility, and correcting any incorrect running mechanics.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 2 weeks for re-evaluation. }}<|eot_id|>
